.idea
bin
obj

# Rider per-user solution settings (machine-local; must never be committed)
*.DotSettings.user

# Test-run output: `dotnet test --logger trx` writes tests/**/TestResults/, one folder per run. Never commit it —
# and note it is NOT covered by `bin`/`obj`, so a run that forgets to clean up shows up as untracked.
**/TestResults

# The Linux run directory, as documented in README and docs/build-image.md: a plain `dotnet publish` output, or
# an AppImage build with `--output type=local,dest=dist`. It is a build tree of tens of megabytes with a 25 MB
# AOT binary in it, and it is NOT covered by `bin`/`obj` — so without this line a `git add -A` on a machine that
# has ever built for Linux stages the whole thing.
dist

# The OTHER name the same output takes: the publish command in docs/build-image.md writes `-o publish-linux`,
# and that directory is what gets copied into `dist` afterwards. It is the same 92 MB of build tree as `dist`
# and it was untracked-but-unignored until a `git add -A` in this repo put it in front of a commit: `dist` was
# listed here and this name was not, which is exactly the kind of near-miss a second entry prevents.
publish-linux

# The WINDOWS name for the same output, from README's own publish command (`-o publish`): a ~49 MB single-file
# exe plus three .pdb files. Same class of near-miss as the entry above, with one extra wrinkle that hid it
# longer — the Windows publish needs an MSVC linker and a Windows SDK, so on a machine without them the directory
# stays EMPTY and git never reports it. It appeared the first time a publish actually succeeded here.
publish
